Article 1: Where an objector raises an enforcement objection or an applicant for reconsideration applies for reconsideration, the objector or applicant shall submit a written application to the People's Court. The application shall specify the specific objection or reconsideration request, facts, reasons, and other matters, and shall be accompanied by the following materials:
(1) identification of the objector or the applicant for reconsideration;
(2) relevant evidentiary materials;
(3) service address and contact information.

Article 5: Under any of the following circumstances, a natural person, legal person, or unincorporated organization other than a party may act as an interested party to raise an enforcement act objection:
(1) where it considers that an enforcement act of the People's Court is illegal and obstructs the satisfaction of its claims secured by queued seizure, attachment, or freezing;
(2) where it considers that an auction measure of the People's Court is illegal and obstructs its participation in fair bidding;
(3) where it considers that auction, forced sale, or satisfaction of a debt by transfer of property adopted by the People's Court is illegal and infringes its right of first refusal over the subject matter of enforcement;
(4) where it considers that the matters requested by the People's Court for assistance in enforcement exceed its assistance scope or violate legal provisions; or
(5) where it considers that other lawful rights and interests have been infringed by an illegal enforcement act of the People's Court.

Article 7: Where a party or interested party considers that any of the following acts during the enforcement process or during the process of enforcing a ruling on preservation or advance execution is illegal and raises an objection, the People's Court shall review the objection pursuant to Article 225 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) enforcement measures such as seizure, attachment, freezing, auction, forced sale, satisfaction of debt by transfer of property, suspension of enforcement, stay of enforcement, or conclusion of enforcement;
(2) legal procedures to be observed such as the time limits and sequence of enforcement;
(3) other acts performed by the People's Court that infringe the lawful rights and interests of a party or interested party.
Where the party subject to enforcement raises an objection to exclude enforcement based on substantive matters arising after the enforcement instrument takes effect, such as extinguishment of the claim or loss of the force of compulsory execution, the People's Court shall review the objection with reference to Article 225 of the Civil Procedure Law.
Except for the circumstances provided in Article 19 of these Provisions, where the party subject to enforcement raises an objection to exclude enforcement based on substantive matters arising before the enforcement instrument takes effect, the People's Court shall inform the party to apply for a retrial according to law or to resolve the matter through other procedures.

Article 17: The People's Court shall handle an enforcement act objection according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the objection is not established, rule to dismiss the objection;
(2) if the objection is established, rule to revoke the relevant enforcement act;
(3) if the objection is partially established, rule to modify the relevant enforcement act;
(4) if the objection is established or partially established, but the enforcement act contains nothing to be revoked or modified, rule that the objection or the corresponding part of the objection is established.

Article 21: Where a party or interested party raises an objection requesting the revocation of an auction, and any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall support the request:
(1) bidders collude maliciously among themselves, or bidders and the auction institution collude maliciously, harming the interests of a party or other bidders;
(2) the buyer does not have the bidding qualification prescribed by law;
(3) bidders are illegally restricted from participating in the bidding, or different bidding conditions are prescribed for different bidders;
(4) the auction subject matter is not publicly announced in accordance with the provisions of laws and judicial interpretations; or
(5) other circumstances that seriously violate the auction procedures and harm the interests of a party or bidders.
Where a party or interested party requests revocation of a forced sale, the preceding paragraph shall be applied by reference.

Article 23: After reviewing an application for reconsideration against an objection ruling, the People's Court at the next higher level shall handle the case according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the facts found in the objection ruling are clear, the law is correctly applied, and the result should be maintained, it shall rule to dismiss the reconsideration application and maintain the objection ruling;
(2) if the facts found in the objection ruling are erroneous or the law is incorrectly applied, and the result should be corrected, it shall rule to revoke or modify the objection ruling;
(3) if the basic facts found in the objection ruling are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, it shall rule to revoke the objection ruling and remand the case to the People's Court that made the ruling for new review, or make a corresponding ruling after ascertaining the facts;
(4) if the objection ruling omits objection requests or has other circumstances seriously violating statutory procedures, it shall rule to revoke the objection ruling and remand the case to the People's Court that made the ruling for new review;
(5) if the objection ruling, for an objection that should have been reviewed and handled pursuant to Article 227 of the Civil Procedure Law, erroneously applies Article 225 of the Civil Procedure Law to review and handle it, it shall rule to revoke the objection ruling and remand the case to the People's Court that made the ruling for a new ruling.
Except for the circumstances of remanding for new review or making a new ruling under items 3, 4, and 5 of the first paragraph of this Article, where a ruling to revoke or modify the objection ruling is made and the enforcement act can be revoked or modified, the enforcement act maintained by the objection ruling shall also be revoked or modified at the same time.
After the People's Court makes a ruling on a case remanded for new review, if a party or interested party applies for reconsideration, the higher People's Court shall not remand the case for new review again after reconsideration.

Article 25: With respect to an objection raised by a person not involved in the case, the People's Court shall determine whether the person is the right holder in accordance with the following standards:
(1) for registered immovable property, determination shall be made in accordance with the immovable property register; for unregistered buildings, structures, and their ancillary facilities, determination shall be made in accordance with relevant evidence such as the land use right register, construction project planning permit, and construction permit;
(2) for registered specific movable property such as motor vehicles, ships, and aircraft, determination shall be made in accordance with the registration of the relevant administrative department; for unregistered specific movable property and other movable property, determination shall be made in accordance with actual possession;
(3) for bank deposits and securities deposited with financial institutions, determination shall be made in accordance with the account names registered with the financial institution or the registration and clearing institution; where securities are held in the name of a custodian institution lawfully qualified for operation, determination shall be made in accordance with the account name of the actual contributor registered by the institution;
(4) for equity interests, determination shall be made in accordance with the registration of the administrative authority for industry and commerce and the information published by the enterprise credit information publicity system; and
(5) for other property and rights, where there is registration, determination shall be made in accordance with the registration of the registration institution; where there is no registration, determination shall be made in accordance with evidence proving the ownership of the property or the right holder, such as contracts.
Where a person not involved in the case raises an objection to exclude enforcement based on a lawful instrument that has taken effect in another case, and the right holder of the subject matter of enforcement determined by that instrument is inconsistent with the determination made in accordance with the preceding paragraph, the matter shall be handled in accordance with Article 26 of these Provisions.

Article 28: In the enforcement of a monetary claim, where a buyer raises an objection to immovable property registered in the name of the party subject to enforcement, and the following circumstances are met and the buyer's right can exclude enforcement, the People's Court shall support the objection:
(1) a lawful and valid written sales contract has been signed before the seizure by the People's Court;
(2) the buyer has lawfully taken possession of the immovable property before the seizure by the People's Court;
(3) the full price has been paid, or part of the price has been paid in accordance with the contract and the remaining price has been delivered for enforcement in accordance with the requirements of the People's Court; and
(4) the failure to complete the registration of transfer of ownership is not due to the buyer's own reasons.
